# .release-removals — deliberate deletions against the PUBLISHED tree.
#
# One path or path-prefix per line; `#` comments allowed. The `[6/6] public-regress`
# gate blocks any release that removes a file already on public `master` unless it is
# declared here. A deletion is not forbidden — it must be intended, and this file is
# the reviewed record that it was. Read by dev-pipeline/promote/promote.sh.
#
# A comment-only or blank file is NOT an allow-all; it declares nothing.

# ── The crypto-agility corpus is de-versioned ──────────────────────────────────
# The corpus had two committed copies — a revision-stamped working copy under
# `specs/test-vectors/v767/` and a de-versioned publish form under
# `crypto-agility/` — and they collapse into one. The published `-v1` names go with
# it. The artifact is byte-neutral across the rename:
# b5484e84dd2cddfa7d3cc8a041deba92cb29615aedb2180e31d8b6910ac5b648, 10874 B,
# measured on both sides. `agility-SEEDS.md` -> `SEEDS.md` is a rename only.
#
# A reader loses nothing: the same three files are published under the new names,
# `crypto-agility/README.md` points at them, and `crypto-agility/CHANGELOG.md` (new,
# declared canonical) carries the corpus history back to the first byte-pin.
#
# DECLARE ALL THREE EVEN THOUGH THE GATE ONLY FLAGS ONE. `public_regression_ok`
# runs `git diff --diff-filter=D` with rename detection at its default (ON), so in
# this tree only the `.cbor` surfaces as a deletion — the `.md` and `.diag` pair as
# renames and pass silently. That is the gate under-reporting, not a smaller
# removal. Declaring only what the gate happens to see would make this file a record
# of the tool's blind spot rather than of our intent.
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-SEEDS.md
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-vectors-v1.cbor
specs/test-vectors/crypto-agility/agility-vectors-v1.diag

# ── [ADR-0031] — rolling status docs are not a publication surface ─────────────
# `docs/status/STATUS.md` has been public since the last release and its `[[doc]]`
# block is removed from CANONICAL-DOCS.toml in the same commit as this line.
#
# Why: a rolling status file is working memory — dated measurements, seat names,
# blockers, half-verified numbers — and [ADR-0031] rules that `docs/status/`
# publishes nothing, `STATUS.md` included. Keeping it public is what forces pin
# hygiene and audience-shaped prose onto a file whose whole value is being frank.
#
# What a reader loses: nothing they came for. README.md answers "what is this",
# CHANGELOG.md answers "what changed", and the specs themselves are the artifact —
# all declared canonical and written for that reader. Reversible: restore the
# `[[doc]]` block and drop this line.
#
# Prefix, not just the one file: the dated HANDOFF-*/CHECKPOINT-*/PAUSED-* snapshots
# beside it are internal too, and none should reach public master again.
docs/status
